  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Implementing Inclusive Education for Blind Children in Bombali Sierra (Sierra Le</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Sierra Leone very few blind or visually impaired children go to school. Parents do not see the need for it; schools do not have the facilities or equipment required to teach them; and teachers are not trained in working with children with disabilities. Prejudice is rife and decision-makers often ignorant about the issues. However, Sierra Leone has recently passed new Disability Legislation and a new National Disability Commission is being established to implement the law. This project focuses on supporting blind children to go to school. It will provide practical training for teachers, will work with families and children to support them into schools, will educate communities about the rights of blind children, and will equip six mainstream schools with the skills and training to educate and support blind or visually impaired (BVI) children. Children from specialist blind schools, as well as those who have for so long been denied an education due to their disability, will be able to learn alongside their peers in mainstream schools. The project will provide a blueprint for initiatives across Sierra Leone so that all BVI children can access the education to which they are entitled.</narrative>
  </description>
